# No Runtime CreatePrimitive

Do **not** call `GameObject.CreatePrimitive` in runtime gameplay code. Author meshes, materials, colliders, and transforms on **prefabs or scene objects** instead; instantiate or toggle them at runtime.

## Why

- **Not editable** — geometry built from `CreatePrimitive` exists only in C#; artists and designers cannot tune it in the Inspector (see scene-inspector-authoring).
- **Hidden colliders** — every primitive auto-adds a collider (`BoxCollider`, `SphereCollider`, `CapsuleCollider`, or `MeshCollider`). Code usually destroys it immediately, wasting work and inviting bugs.
- **WebGL stripping** — with Strip Engine Code on, the auto-added collider type must be statically referenced or `AddComponent` throws at runtime. Banning `CreatePrimitive` removes this footgun entirely.

## Banned in runtime

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — runtime primitive construction
var go = GameObject.CreatePrimitive(PrimitiveType.Capsule);
Destroy(go.GetComponent<CapsuleCollider>());
```

## Use instead

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD — prefab authored in Editor; runtime only instantiates
[SerializeField] private GameObject _placeholderPrefab;

private void SpawnPlaceholder(Transform parent)
{
    Instantiate(_placeholderPrefab, parent);
}
```

For static scene geometry, place the object in the scene/prefab hierarchy and hold a `[SerializeField]` reference — no spawn needed.

## Allowed

- **Editor scripts** (`Editor/` folders, editor asmdefs) — setup/migration tooling.
- **Tests** — unless the project bans it there too.

`RuntimeCreatePrimitiveGuard` (build preprocess in the shared build-guard assembly, `MonkeyTilt.Shared.Guards.Editor`) fails **all** player builds when runtime code calls `CreatePrimitive`.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] No `GameObject.CreatePrimitive` in runtime scripts
- [ ] New 3D chrome/placeholders authored as prefab or scene object
- [ ] Runtime code instantiates or enables authored objects; does not construct Unity primitives from code
